all right so I quit my job I quit my job  as an associate Solutions architect  working at AWS this decision was not an  easy one at all because in AWS I really  liked my team I like the work I was  doing and for the most part I had a  great time it felt like almost 10 years  in the company even though I've only  been there for two and a half years and  that just goes to show how much I was  able to learn and develop and build up  my technical skills as well as make an  impact on things that I was passionate  about I got to help out a lot of  customers and I also got to start  programs internally within the company  on early career initiatives and women in  Tech and so I really found it hard to  say goodbye to everyone but the reason I  decided to leave is to actually do  YouTube full-time this YouTube channel  is almost like my second identity so  five days a week I was a Solutions  architect and on the weekends I would be  Tech with Lucy and record videos write  scripts and edit and publish them so it  was almost as if I had two identities  for the past two years in my career and  what I really liked is how these two  identities really complemented Each  Other Well my YouTube channel helped me  with my personal branding at work and it  was great to meet people at work who  have come across a child and go like  wait you're taking Lucy I thought that  was pretty cool and also what I was  learning at work and my perspective as a  Solutions architect was really helpful  in making videos for the tech with Lucy  Channel and in turn that helped a lot of  people land a job in the cloud industry  and helped people get a degree certified  and build the technical skills as you  can tell I really liked both sides of  this two-part identity so both the  solutions Arctic role and my role as a  content creator on YouTube and so for  the longest time I did both I worked  seven days a week and whenever I had  free time after work I would plan out  the next video idea but what ended up  happening was as my responsibilities I  worked continued to increase and my  YouTube videos continue to require more  effort and time as well I found it  extremely hard to balance both it came  to a point where I really had to just  take a step back and ask myself do I  want YouTube to be a side Hobby and  something that I'll continue to do  alongside my role or do I potentially  want to do YouTube full-time  so here comes the question why did I  choose to do YouTube full-time as  opposed to keeping it as a side Hobby  and focusing on my main career well  first of all YouTube is just really  really fun for me I really like the  creative process behind making videos  you know storytelling thinking about a  way to teach something in a way that's  easy to understand it's something that I  really enjoy that whole process but also  when people watch the videos and get  value out of it the cool thing about  YouTube compared to other platforms is  that once you make a video it can  continue to show up in search for months  and years afterwards and that brings me  on to the second reason potential and  impact what I feel like I'm doing with  YouTube is I'm building a repository of  information to help people build their  technical skills and land a job in the  cloud industry I base all of my videos  off of suggestions from all of you so  you know when you leave a comment down  below and let me know what type of  videos you want to see I looked through  the videos I find the most commonly  asked questions and I make a video about  it so over the past two years of making  videos my viewers actually want to see I  feel like I've really built trust in the  tech with Lucy community and when I  decided to quit my job the channel was  growing at around a thousand subscribers  a week I really believe there's  potential to do even more whether that's  making videos more frequently on YouTube  or branching out into other platforms  like Tick Tock or Instagram there's just  so many ideas going around in my head  all the time and I wanted to dedicate  some time to work on YouTube full-time  so that I could actually flesh out some  of these ideas and turn it into reality  the final reason is money and this blows  my mind to say but currently the YouTube  business pays me more than what I was  getting at AWS so part of the money is  from YouTube themselves when they show  ads before my videos and at the end or  maybe in the middle as well and the main  portion comes from Brand deals and  sponsorships so if you've seen me  promote a product maybe like a free AWS  course these companies I collaborate  with would pay me a one-off fee so with  this business model I'll be able to  continue to make free content for the  community whilst having a full-time  income  as I was recording this video someone  sent me a message they asked me don't  you think it was a mistake to leave AWS  I mean how you feel is important of  course but to me you're leaving a  position that many people dream of but  will never reach a position with  security and benefits essentially you  know why leave for a trending YouTube  channel this is quite interesting and a  few people have asked me this already  YouTube yeah it might not be around  forever and there's no guarantee that my  YouTube business will just keep growing  and you know continue to succeed at some  point there may be a decline or maybe at  some point I want to go back to having a  job in the tech industry during my time  at AWS I learned about something called  a one-way door and a two-way door a  one-way door is essentially a decision  where once you make the decision you can  never reverse it and you never go back  to before you made the decision whereas  a two-way door is something where you  make the decision you can trial it out  make a few mistakes and if or else fails  you could go back to where you started  or you can take the learnings and choose  a different path without causing much  damage so I view my decision as a  two-way door I walk in and then I can  come out if it's not right for me or if  something goes wrong I think what's  important as well is just knowing your  value I know that the skills I developed  through this whole YouTube Journey will  still be valuable in the tech industry  if I decide to go back and these types  of risky moves are probably better to do  earlier on in a career as well I've just  turned 23 this year and for example if I  do YouTube for three years I can still  go back into the tech industry and  restart my career  a big thank you to everyone who has been  a part of this journey whether you've  been watching my videos for a while or  maybe you decided to subscribe because  you saw my Twitter post about me leaving  AWS the channel would not be where it is  now without everyone's support I want to  end this video by saying I'm very very  grateful for this opportunity and I know  I'm at a very privileged place to just  quit my job and not worry about the  financial side and the long-term future  of my career as always please give this  video a like to help out the channel and  I'll see you in the comments below bye  for now
 

